1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,213
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:04,213 --> 00:00:08,768
Principles, Big Idea 2: Data. Let's see
how computers store data, shrink it, and

3
00:00:08,768 --> 00:00:13,266
turn it into information. After every
lesson, we'll work through a full example

4
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:17,024
step by step, and we'll finish by putting
the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,197 --> 00:00:22,336
Everything a computer stores, numbers,
text, pictures, is made of bits.

6
00:00:22,786 --> 00:00:27,746
Big Idea 2 starts with how bits represent
numbers, and what happens when they run

7
00:00:27,746 --> 00:00:27,991
out.

8
00:00:29,141 --> 00:00:32,085
A bit is a zero or a one. A byte is eight
bits.

9
00:00:32,535 --> 00:00:37,885
In binary, each place is worth twice the
place to its right: one, two, four, eight,

10
00:00:37,885 --> 00:00:38,530
and so on.

11
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:43,818
To convert binary to decimal, add the
place values where there's a one.

12
00:00:44,268 --> 00:00:48,172
One zero one one is eight plus two plus
one, which is eleven.

13
00:00:49,322 --> 00:00:52,309
With n bits, you can make two to the n
different patterns.

14
00:00:52,759 --> 00:00:57,958
Three bits give eight patterns, zero to
seven. Eight bits give two hundred fifty

15
00:00:57,958 --> 00:00:59,778
six, zero to two fifty five.

16
00:01:00,928 --> 00:01:04,042
Many languages store numbers in a fixed
number of bits.

17
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:09,599
If a result is too big to fit, you get an
overflow error. With four bits, fifteen

18
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:12,940
plus one doesn't fit, because the largest
is fifteen.

19
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:19,043
Bits are also used for rounding. Some
numbers, like one third, can't be stored

20
00:01:19,043 --> 00:01:22,218
exactly, so computers store a close
approximation.

21
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:28,340
That's why point one plus point two can
come out as point three zero zero zero

22
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:29,794
zero zero zero four.

23
00:01:30,944 --> 00:01:34,506
Your turn. How many different values can
be stored with six bits?

24
00:01:34,956 --> 00:01:36,151
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:36,601 --> 00:01:40,974
Two to the sixth, which is sixty four
values, from zero to sixty three.

26
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:44,411
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

27
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:50,169
Let's convert a number to eight bit
binary, then watch an addition overflow.

28
00:01:51,319 --> 00:01:56,205
The problem: write one hundred fifty six
in eight bit binary. Then, in an eight bit

29
00:01:56,205 --> 00:01:58,914
counter, what is two hundred plus one
hundred?

30
00:02:00,064 --> 00:02:04,841
The place values are one twenty eight,
sixty four, thirty two, sixteen, eight,

31
00:02:04,841 --> 00:02:05,760
four, two, one.

32
00:02:06,210 --> 00:02:11,533
Take the biggest place value that fits,
subtract, and repeat. Eight bits can hold

33
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:13,570
zero to two hundred fifty five.

34
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:19,856
One twenty eight fits: one. Twenty eight
left. Sixty four, thirty two: zero.

35
00:02:19,856 --> 00:02:21,952
Sixteen fits: one, twelve left.

36
00:02:22,402 --> 00:02:28,680
Eight fits: one, four left. Four fits:
one, zero left. Two and one: zero. So one

37
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:31,191
zero zero one one one zero zero.

38
00:02:31,641 --> 00:02:36,581
Two hundred plus one hundred is three
hundred, more than two fifty five. The

39
00:02:36,581 --> 00:02:41,326
extra bit is lost, leaving three hundred
minus two fifty six: forty four.

40
00:02:42,476 --> 00:02:48,400
One hundred fifty six is one zero zero one
one one zero zero, and the eight bit sum

41
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,327
wraps around to forty four.

42
00:02:50,777 --> 00:02:56,049
Each extra bit doubles how many values you
can store. n bits store two to the n

43
00:02:56,049 --> 00:02:56,516
values.

44
00:02:57,666 --> 00:03:02,594
Your turn. What decimal number is one zero
one one zero one zero one?

45
00:03:03,044 --> 00:03:04,238
Pause the video now.

46
00:03:04,688 --> 00:03:09,431
One eighty one: one twenty eight plus
thirty two plus sixteen plus four plus

47
00:03:09,431 --> 00:03:09,680
one.

48
00:03:10,130 --> 00:03:11,261
Next, compression.

49
00:03:12,411 --> 00:03:17,380
A raw photo from your phone can be dozens
of megabytes. Compression makes files

50
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:20,902
smaller, so they're faster to send and
cheaper to store.

51
00:03:21,352 --> 00:03:22,461
There are two kinds.

52
00:03:23,611 --> 00:03:28,347
Lossless compression shrinks a file so it
can be restored exactly, bit for bit.

53
00:03:28,797 --> 00:03:34,706
One simple method is run length encoding.
A A A A B B B becomes four A, three B.

54
00:03:35,856 --> 00:03:40,830
Lossless compression is needed when every
bit matters: text documents, code, and

55
00:03:40,830 --> 00:03:41,638
spreadsheets.

56
00:03:42,088 --> 00:03:44,178
Zip files use lossless compression.

57
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:50,064
Lossy compression throws away some data
that people are unlikely to notice.

58
00:03:50,514 --> 00:03:55,016
It makes files much smaller, but the
original can never be fully restored.

59
00:03:56,166 --> 00:04:01,627
Photos, music and video often use lossy
compression, like JPEG and MP3.

60
00:04:02,077 --> 00:04:06,429
A slightly blurrier photo is usually fine,
if it's ten times smaller.

61
00:04:07,579 --> 00:04:12,763
On the AP exam, choose lossless when the
original must be reconstructed exactly.

62
00:04:13,213 --> 00:04:17,864
Choose lossy when smaller size matters
most, and some quality loss is OK.

63
00:04:19,014 --> 00:04:24,646
Your turn. Use run length encoding to
compress W W W W W B B W.

64
00:04:25,096 --> 00:04:26,290
Pause the video now.

65
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:28,938
Five W, two B, one W.

66
00:04:29,388 --> 00:04:32,374
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

67
00:04:33,525 --> 00:04:38,063
Let's compress a message with run length
encoding, then decide when lossy

68
00:04:38,063 --> 00:04:39,306
compression is okay.

69
00:04:40,456 --> 00:04:46,370
The problem: compress A A A A B B B C C D
with run length encoding. How much smaller

70
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:46,792
is it?

71
00:04:47,242 --> 00:04:49,696
Then pick lossless or lossy for three
files.

72
00:04:50,846 --> 00:04:55,811
Run length encoding writes each run as a
count followed by the character. It's

73
00:04:55,811 --> 00:04:58,675
lossless: you can rebuild the exact
original.

74
00:04:59,825 --> 00:05:05,670
Four A's become four A. Three B's become
three B. Two C's, two C. One D, one D.

75
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:10,111
The original has ten characters, the
compressed has eight. That's eighty

76
00:05:10,111 --> 00:05:11,219
percent of the size.

77
00:05:11,669 --> 00:05:17,753
A bank statement must be exact: lossless.
A vacation photo can lose tiny details:

78
00:05:17,753 --> 00:05:22,485
lossy is fine. A song for streaming: lossy
saves a lot of data.

79
00:05:23,635 --> 00:05:27,347
Four A three B two C one D: ten characters
down to eight.

80
00:05:27,797 --> 00:05:32,739
Lossless keeps every bit. Lossy throws
away detail people won't notice, so it

81
00:05:32,739 --> 00:05:35,370
gets much smaller, but you can't undo it.

82
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:40,275
Your turn. Does run length encoding make A
B C D smaller?

83
00:05:40,725 --> 00:05:41,920
Pause the video now.

84
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:48,380
No. It becomes one A one B one C one D,
eight characters instead of four. RLE only

85
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:49,772
helps with repeats.

86
00:05:50,222 --> 00:05:52,590
Next, extracting information from data.

87
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:57,708
Data is just raw values. Information is
what we learn from it.

88
00:05:58,158 --> 00:06:02,510
Computers help us turn huge amounts of
data into useful information.

89
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:07,052
A store records every sale: the item, the
price, the time.

90
00:06:07,502 --> 00:06:12,792
One sale tells you little. A million sales
can show which items are popular, and when

91
00:06:12,792 --> 00:06:13,539
people shop.

92
00:06:14,689 --> 00:06:17,015
Patterns in data can lead to new
knowledge.

93
00:06:17,465 --> 00:06:22,754
But a pattern, or correlation, does not
prove that one thing causes another. Ice

94
00:06:22,754 --> 00:06:28,110
cream sales and sunburns rise together,
but ice cream doesn't cause sunburns. Hot

95
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:29,433
weather causes both.

96
00:06:30,583 --> 00:06:34,103
Big data sets are often too large to
explore by hand.

97
00:06:34,553 --> 00:06:39,655
Programs can sort, filter and summarize
them, and scale to handle more data with

98
00:06:39,655 --> 00:06:40,611
more computers.

99
00:06:41,761 --> 00:06:46,676
Data can also be misleading. If it's
incomplete, or collected from only one

100
00:06:46,676 --> 00:06:49,100
group, the conclusions can be biased.

101
00:06:49,550 --> 00:06:53,113
More data doesn't fix bias if it's the
same kind of data.

102
00:06:54,263 --> 00:06:59,149
So when you see a claim based on data,
ask: where did the data come from, who is

103
00:06:59,149 --> 00:07:01,836
missing, and is it a correlation or a
cause?

104
00:07:02,986 --> 00:07:07,771
Your turn. A study finds students who eat
breakfast get higher grades. Does that

105
00:07:07,771 --> 00:07:09,983
prove breakfast causes higher grades?

106
00:07:10,433 --> 00:07:11,628
Pause the video now.

107
00:07:12,078 --> 00:07:16,942
No. It's a correlation. Something else,
like sleep or routine, could explain both.

108
00:07:17,392 --> 00:07:20,379
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

109
00:07:21,529 --> 00:07:26,194
Let's look for a pattern in a small data
set, and avoid the most common mistake in

110
00:07:26,194 --> 00:07:26,820
reading it.

111
00:07:27,970 --> 00:07:32,974
The problem: an ice cream stand recorded
the temperature and sales for five days.

112
00:07:32,974 --> 00:07:37,484
What pattern do you see? Does hot weather
cause sales, or something else?

113
00:07:38,634 --> 00:07:41,194
Sort the rows by temperature and look at
sales.

114
00:07:41,644 --> 00:07:46,304
A correlation means two things change
together. It doesn't prove one causes the

115
00:07:46,304 --> 00:07:46,658
other.

116
00:07:47,808 --> 00:07:53,348
Sorted by temperature: sixty two, forty
sales. Sixty eight, sixty. Seventy five,

117
00:07:53,348 --> 00:07:57,365
eighty five. Eighty one, one ten. Eighty
eight, one forty.

118
00:07:57,815 --> 00:08:01,975
As temperature goes up, sales go up.
That's a positive correlation.

119
00:08:02,425 --> 00:08:07,660
It's very likely hot weather causes it
here. But the data alone can't prove it:

120
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:09,913
maybe Thursday was also a holiday.

121
00:08:11,063 --> 00:08:14,370
Sales rise with temperature: a positive
correlation.

122
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:19,857
To be more sure, collect more days, and
check other factors. Correlation is not

123
00:08:19,857 --> 00:08:20,494
causation.

124
00:08:21,644 --> 00:08:26,676
Your turn. Towns with more firefighters
have more fires. Do firefighters cause

125
00:08:26,676 --> 00:08:27,063
fires?

126
00:08:27,513 --> 00:08:28,708
Pause the video now.

127
00:08:29,158 --> 00:08:34,449
No. Bigger towns have both more fires and
more firefighters. Size is the hidden

128
00:08:34,449 --> 00:08:34,918
factor.

129
00:08:35,368 --> 00:08:37,224
Next, using programs with data.

130
00:08:38,373 --> 00:08:41,744
Before data can answer questions, it
usually needs work.

131
00:08:42,194 --> 00:08:45,372
Programs help us clean it, filter it, and
visualize it.

132
00:08:46,522 --> 00:08:49,040
Cleaning data makes it uniform and
correct.

133
00:08:49,490 --> 00:08:55,079
For example, a survey has the answers N Y,
New York, and new york. Cleaning turns

134
00:08:55,079 --> 00:09:00,185
them all into New York. Cleaning also
removes duplicates and fixes obvious

135
00:09:00,185 --> 00:09:00,668
errors.

136
00:09:01,818 --> 00:09:04,357
Filtering keeps only the rows you care
about.

137
00:09:04,807 --> 00:09:08,540
From a table of students, filter to only
the ones in grade ten.

138
00:09:09,690 --> 00:09:14,394
Then you can transform and summarize:
count, total, average, or find the

139
00:09:14,394 --> 00:09:14,917
maximum.

140
00:09:15,367 --> 00:09:20,173
What's the average score of grade ten
students? A program can answer that in a

141
00:09:20,173 --> 00:09:22,514
split second, even for a million rows.

142
00:09:23,664 --> 00:09:26,053
Visualizing turns numbers into charts.

143
00:09:26,503 --> 00:09:31,572
A bar chart compares categories. A line
chart shows change over time. Patterns

144
00:09:31,572 --> 00:09:34,887
that are hidden in a table can jump out in
a chart.

145
00:09:36,037 --> 00:09:40,638
Tools like spreadsheets, and programs you
write yourself, let you repeat these

146
00:09:40,638 --> 00:09:42,821
steps, and ask new questions quickly.

147
00:09:43,971 --> 00:09:49,299
Your turn. A data set has the colors red,
Red, and RED. Which step fixes this:

148
00:09:49,299 --> 00:09:51,758
cleaning, filtering, or visualizing?

149
00:09:52,208 --> 00:09:53,402
Pause the video now.

150
00:09:53,852 --> 00:09:58,482
Cleaning. It makes the values uniform, so
they're all counted as the same color.

151
00:09:58,932 --> 00:10:01,918
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

152
00:10:03,069 --> 00:10:07,252
Let's clean a messy data set before
counting it, so the program gives the

153
00:10:07,252 --> 00:10:07,997
right answer.

154
00:10:09,147 --> 00:10:14,568
The problem: a club survey has answers
like Robotics, robotics with a space, and

155
00:10:14,568 --> 00:10:17,211
ROBOTICS. One row is missing its hours.

156
00:10:17,661 --> 00:10:19,517
How many students chose each club?

157
00:10:20,667 --> 00:10:26,377
Cleaning makes data uniform. Trim spaces,
and use the same capitalization for every

158
00:10:26,377 --> 00:10:26,790
entry.

159
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:30,760
Rows with missing values are removed, or
handled separately.

160
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:37,271
Before cleaning, a program would see three
different clubs: Robotics, robotics with a

161
00:10:37,271 --> 00:10:39,163
space, and ROBOTICS. One each.

162
00:10:39,613 --> 00:10:43,005
After cleaning, all three become Robotics:
three members.

163
00:10:43,455 --> 00:10:47,914
Fay's row is missing hours, so it's
skipped. Seven of eight rows remain.

164
00:10:49,064 --> 00:10:53,074
Robotics has three members, not one.
Cleaning changed the answer.

165
00:10:53,524 --> 00:10:59,035
Programs let you filter, clean, sort and
combine data much faster than by hand, but

166
00:10:59,035 --> 00:11:01,823
only clean data gives trustworthy results.

167
00:11:02,973 --> 00:11:07,965
Your turn. A list of states has NY, New
York, and ny. What should cleaning do?

168
00:11:08,415 --> 00:11:09,610
Pause the video now.

169
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:13,025
Turn them all into one standard form, like
NY.

170
00:11:13,475 --> 00:11:14,606
Next, metadata.

171
00:11:15,755 --> 00:11:18,422
Every photo you take stores more than the
picture.

172
00:11:18,872 --> 00:11:22,434
It also stores data about the picture.
That's metadata.

173
00:11:23,584 --> 00:11:25,526
Metadata is data about data.

174
00:11:25,976 --> 00:11:31,031
For a photo, metadata can include the
date, the time, the camera model, the

175
00:11:31,031 --> 00:11:33,592
image size, and even the GPS location.

176
00:11:34,742 --> 00:11:37,515
Metadata helps us organize and find data.

177
00:11:37,965 --> 00:11:43,667
Your phone uses it to group photos by date
and place. Search engines use it to find

178
00:11:43,667 --> 00:11:45,453
songs by artist and album.

179
00:11:46,603 --> 00:11:50,507
Changing or deleting metadata doesn't
change the main data.

180
00:11:50,957 --> 00:11:55,992
If you remove the location from a photo,
the picture itself looks exactly the same.

181
00:11:57,142 --> 00:12:02,304
But metadata can reveal a lot. A photo's
location can show where someone lives.

182
00:12:02,754 --> 00:12:07,000
Before you post photos publicly, consider
turning off location data.

183
00:12:08,150 --> 00:12:13,244
On the AP exam, look for questions that
ask which question can be answered using

184
00:12:13,244 --> 00:12:16,683
only the metadata, without looking at the
data itself.

185
00:12:17,833 --> 00:12:22,900
Your turn. A music file's metadata has the
title, artist, and length. Can you use

186
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:27,904
only the metadata to find which songs are
longer than four minutes? Can you find

187
00:12:27,904 --> 00:12:29,844
which songs have a guitar solo?

188
00:12:30,294 --> 00:12:31,488
Pause the video now.

189
00:12:31,938 --> 00:12:36,611
Yes for the length. No for the guitar
solo: that's in the sound itself, not the

190
00:12:36,611 --> 00:12:37,144
metadata.

191
00:12:37,594 --> 00:12:40,580
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

192
00:12:41,731 --> 00:12:46,351
Let's separate a photo's data from its
metadata, and see what the metadata

193
00:12:46,351 --> 00:12:46,851
reveals.

194
00:12:48,001 --> 00:12:52,991
The problem: you post a photo of your dog.
Which parts are data, which are metadata,

195
00:12:52,991 --> 00:12:54,892
and what could a stranger learn?

196
00:12:56,042 --> 00:13:01,434
Data is the content itself: the pixels.
Metadata is data about the data: when,

197
00:13:01,434 --> 00:13:03,231
where and how it was made.

198
00:13:04,381 --> 00:13:08,797
The pixels are the data. Changing the
metadata doesn't change the picture.

199
00:13:09,247 --> 00:13:13,023
The date, camera, size and GPS location
are metadata.

200
00:13:13,473 --> 00:13:18,745
The GPS coordinates show exactly where the
photo was taken. If that's your home,

201
00:13:18,745 --> 00:13:20,854
strangers now know your address.

202
00:13:22,004 --> 00:13:27,338
Metadata helps organize and search files,
like finding all photos from October.

203
00:13:27,788 --> 00:13:32,879
But it can reveal private information.
Turn off location tagging, or remove

204
00:13:32,879 --> 00:13:34,508
metadata before posting.

205
00:13:35,658 --> 00:13:39,604
Your turn. For a song file, is the artist
name data or metadata?

206
00:13:40,054 --> 00:13:41,249
Pause the video now.

207
00:13:41,699 --> 00:13:45,582
Metadata. The audio is the data. The
artist name describes it.

208
00:13:46,032 --> 00:13:48,122
Next, let's put Big Idea two together.

209
00:13:49,272 --> 00:13:53,976
You've learned binary, compression,
extracting information, using programs

210
00:13:53,976 --> 00:13:55,501
with data, and metadata.

211
00:13:55,951 --> 00:14:01,135
Now let's write a complete program that
cleans and analyzes a real looking survey.

212
00:14:02,285 --> 00:14:05,784
Here's the plan: a club survey analyzer,
written in Python.

213
00:14:06,234 --> 00:14:11,266
It cleans the messy answers from the last
example, then counts members and average

214
00:14:11,266 --> 00:14:12,186
hours per club.

215
00:14:13,336 --> 00:14:18,839
Part one: the metadata says who collected
the survey and when. The survey itself is

216
00:14:18,839 --> 00:14:21,955
a list of rows: name, club, and hours per
week.

217
00:14:22,405 --> 00:14:25,349
Notice the messy club names, and Fay's
missing hours.

218
00:14:26,499 --> 00:14:32,090
Part two: clean the data. Skip rows with
missing hours. strip removes spaces, and

219
00:14:32,090 --> 00:14:33,816
title fixes the capitals.

220
00:14:34,266 --> 00:14:39,177
Then count members and add up hours for
each club. A dictionary keeps one counter

221
00:14:39,177 --> 00:14:40,026
per club name.

222
00:14:41,176 --> 00:14:45,272
Part three: print the metadata and the
results per club, with averages.

223
00:14:45,722 --> 00:14:50,309
Finally, filter: build a list of students
who spend four or more hours a week.

224
00:14:51,459 --> 00:14:54,232
Let's run it. Seven of eight rows survive
cleaning.

225
00:14:54,682 --> 00:15:00,202
Robotics has three members averaging four
point seven hours. And Ana, Dee and Eli

226
00:15:00,202 --> 00:15:02,383
